  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday morning through next Thursday)\nIssued at 227 PM EDT Fri Apr 17 2026\n\nThe long term remains largely uneventful though fire weather will \nstill need to be monitored as we remain relatively dry and warm.\n\nOn Sunday, the cold front (mentioned in the short-term) will be \nmoving through parts of Central Georgia. Isolated rain/thunder \nchances will continue to be a possibility through the afternoon but \nlow amounts (~a tenth of an inch or less) will do little to help the \nongoing drought conditions. High pressure builds and nudges eastward \ninto North and Central Georgia by Monday. Dry and near-normal \ntemperatures will kick off the work-week but temperatures are \nforecast to gradually rise through the week as high pressure becomes \nmore centered over the Southeast. Thus, high fire danger appears \nlikely for much of next week and something we will be monitoring in \nthe event fire headlines are needed. \n\nAs the front moves through Sunday, forecast highs will range from \nthe upper 60s to the North and upper 80s across much of Central \nGeorgia. On Monday, forecast highs start off in the upper 70s (60s \nin the  mountains) warming back into the mid to upper 80s areawide \nby Thursday of next week. Morning lows will follow a similar trend \nstarting off cooler early Monday with values in the 40s becoming \nmore mild by Thursday with values in the 50s.",
